From maitra at math.umbc.edu  Wed Aug  1 19:02:45 2001
From: maitra at math.umbc.edu (Ranjan Maitra)
Date: Wed, 01 Aug 2001 15:02:45 -0400
Subject: Summary: equivalence classes
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.33.0108011457430.1575-100000@param.math.umbc.edu>

Thanks a bunch to Nick Ellis, Brian Ripley and Yih Chin who
responded. Professors Brian Ripley and Yih Chin suggested similar
and elegant solutions and I provide the latter's code:

If S is your similarity matrix, then this should give you the
required solution:
> tree = linkage(1-S, "connected")
> ans  = cuttree(tree, h=0.5)
where the answer
> ans
[1] 1 2 1 1 2
gives the equivalence class 'number' that each point falls into.

Another excellent solution was given by Nick Ellis which is as follows:

If you just keep multiplying the matrix 'a' by itself, reducing non zeroes
to 1 at each stage, then eventually the product settles down to a steady
state where each row shows an entire equivalence class. Then you can just
gather them as in the last line below.

a <- matrix(rbinom(100,1,0.1),10,10)
a <- a+t(a)
diag(a) <- 1
a <- (a>0)+0 # a random symmtric similarity matrix of 0's and 1's
aPrev <- a
aNext <- ((aPrev %*% a) >0)+0 # iterate once
while (any(aPrev!=aNext)) { # if not steady, keep iterating
        aPrev <- aNext
        aNext <- ((aPrev %*% a) >0)+0
}
a
aNext
unique(sort(apply(aNext,2,function(x) paste((1:10)[x==1],collapse=","))))

