On Thu, 2 Aug 2001, Stafford Phillip-APS095 wrote:

> S Group;
>
> Thank you all for the extremely fast responses to my inf question, 
> x[!is.finite[x]] works great!  Another basic question:  I want to speed up a 
> conditional function:
> for (i in 1:length(col1)){
>       if (col1[i] == "XX") {col[i]<-NA}
>       }
>
> I've tried to figure a way to use by or one of the apply functions to speed 
> this process up but I'm stumped - I even checked the bright yellow book.

Use indexing:

col1[is.element(col1, "XX")] <- NA

1) Vectorization is the most powerful of the speedups in S, so look to try
it first.  Loops over a vector are almost never required.

2) Use is.element to ensure that there are no problems with NAs in col1.
col1[i] == "XX" is NA if col1[i] is.  In your case this works, but != would
not have worked.  In general, one has to remember S uses three-valued
logic, T, F and NA.

3) In S-PLUS 6 the preferred form is

is.na(col1[is.element(col1, "XX")]) <- T

according to the Green Book.

