From sungm at mail.nih.gov  Fri Aug  3 17:22:09 2001
From: sungm at mail.nih.gov (Sung, Myong-Hee (NCI))
Date: Fri, 03 Aug 2001 13:22:09 -0400
Subject: Re: Strange error from tree()
In-Reply-To: <76D006EB9224D411B12B0008C7C59D960136FCE6@nihexchange9.nih.gov>
Message-ID: <76D006EB9224D411B12B0008C7C59D960136FCE7@nihexchange9.nih.gov>

Great thank-you to Bill Dunlap at Insightful for the precise solution to my
problem.
Here are the original question and his message:

> I got a mysterious error from running command tree() inside my script.
The
> relevant
> part of the code is:
>
>
tree(factor(c(rep("P",nP),rep("N",nN)))~.,data=data.frame(rbind(bind,nonbind
> )))
>
> The error message is "Object "nP" not found".  But nP is clearly defined
and
> has the
> correct value just before tree() is run as can be seen by cat().

tree looks in the columns of data.frame given as the data= argument,
then in the global data bases for when it needs to look up names
in the formula.  If you supply a data= argument it does not look
in the frame of the function calling tree.

One way to do this is
        response <- factor(c(rep("P",nP),rep("N",nN)))
        data <- data.frame(rbind(bind,nonbind), response) # why rbind?
        tree(response~., data=data)

