From phillip.stafford at motorola.com  Fri Aug  3 20:57:59 2001
From: phillip.stafford at motorola.com (Stafford Phillip-APS095)
Date: Fri, 03 Aug 2001 15:57:59 -0500
Subject: discrim answer
Message-ID: <07077F6C2A38D511920800D0B7821B3201E3851E@il06exm01.corp.mot.com>

To S+ group who use discrim();

The way discrim and predict.discrim were predicting less than the total number 
of observations in the test set, the answer was really simple:  na.exclude was 
removing NA's from the training set but the algorithm was not aligning the 
predicted values properly when I inserted the predict.discrim results back into 
the original dataset.  It's a matter of using the row values to place the new 
predictions next to the original values.  The best answer is to use is.finite() 
when selecting the training set.

Phillip Stafford
