From fharrell at virginia.edu  Tue Aug  7 18:20:24 2001
From: fharrell at virginia.edu
Date: Tue, 07 Aug 2001 14:20:24 -0400
Subject: Re: Plea for survey samples
References: <01C11F25.9A1E1BC0.jan.brogger@med.uib.no>
Message-ID: <3B703168.F5CA15FB@virginia.edu>

Dear Jan,

I agree with you that S-Plus should be able to do this in
general.  For simple things, I have several functions 
in the Hmisc library
for weighted computations (quantiles, frequency tables,
ECDFs, mean, SD, etc.) and will someday implement
weights in the Design library.  Clustering is already
handled in Design.  But some users need to be aware
that weighted GLMs result in a loss of efficiency
in general, as compared to incorporating the strata
used for weight construction as covariables, and
conditioning on them.  In general I prefer to use
covariables to make results conditional on such
things as age, race, sex.  Reweighted estimates
may sometimes be obtained after the fact,
if you need them.  Special contrasts
of parameter estimates can sometimes be used to
uncondition on the variables in question.  
The contrast function in Design makes
the latter easy for many cases.  When
after-estimation reweighting can be used, one
advantage it has is the quickness with which
the user may obtain estimates of precisions of
various contrasts.  -Frank

Jan Brogger wrote:
> 
> I've looked around for S-PLUS code that estimates models (e.g. GLM) from
> survey sample data that are weighted. This is an old debate. There doesn't
> seem to be a way to do this. I'd like to plead with some of the hot shots
> and brainiacs on this list. You can do so much with S-PLUS, but you can't
> even handle a simple random survey with different selection probabilities ?
> My data are from a simple random sample postal survey where a simple random
> sample of the initial postal nonresponders were selected for telephone
> followup.
> 
> I've done a lot of work in S-PLUS, but have to convert Stata to estimate
> the final models. S-PLUS is elegant, the code is relatively low
> maintenance, its graphs are production quality, you can interface with
> Latex and PDF. If you want, you can estimate more advanced models including
> overdispersion and GAMs. Stata has neither of these. Stata is fine if you
> have simple models. But including three-way interactions or splines makes
> Stata walk through uncharted territory, and it is just not elegant.
> 
> As a first step, I'd like to propose a group that will add to S-PLUS the
> possibility of doing a GLM with with correct variance estimates that has a)
> unequal selection probabilities (fractional weighting) and/or b)
> clustering. If somebody tell me what to do, and they can look over my
> shoulder, I will write it.
> 
> Some have said that all the survey people use SAS anyway. Well, that may be
> true if you just want GLMs and means and proportions. But shouldn't we
> bring the survey sample people into the S-PLUS fold? There is lots more to
> statistics than this. I'm sure that the leading edge of survey sampling
> people will take up on S-PLUS and use its powers. Then this will "trickle
> down" to the grunts (like me) in time.
