From sebuttre at nps.navy.mil  Thu Aug  9 21:20:35 2001
From: sebuttre at nps.navy.mil (Buttrey, Samuel)
Date: Thu, 09 Aug 2001 14:20:35 -0700
Subject: Weird class/method bug/feature?
Message-ID: <21255707C24105438C5BEA7061D33228332D92@essex.ad.nps.navy.mil>

Hi. Here's something weird I discovered; I'm using S-Plus 2000 Professional
Release 3 under Windows NT.

Consider this function, which produces an object of class "thang," and its
associated print method:

> make.thang
function(arg)
{
        out <- 1:3
        class(out) <- "thang"
        cat("Return from make.thang!\n")
        out
}
> print.thang
function(thing, do.something = F)
{
        if(do.something)
                new.thing <- thing    # <- this is the key line
        cat("Print thang!\n")   #
        invisible(return())
}

When I create a "thang" object and print it, either explicitly or by
returning it, all is well:

> make.thang ()
Return from make.thang!
Print thang!
> my.thang <- make.thang ()
Return from make.thang!
> print (my.thang)
Print thang!
> print (my.thang, T)   # <- "do.something" argument has no effect
Print thang!

But when I create the object "on the fly" and pass it to print, then
something different happens. As soon as I access the "thing" item inside the
print.thang() function, make.thang() gets executed a second time. If I never
touch thing, there's no problem, but I can't touch it in any way (to find
out, for example, if it's an expression or a list or what) without it
kicking off another evaluation of make.thang. Check this out:

> print (make.thang() )
Return from make.thang!
Print thang!                               # Okay, that's what I expect
> print (make.thang() , T)           # Pass in "do.something"....
Return from make.thang!
Return from make.thang!           # ...and make.thang() gets called twice.
Print thang!

This is more annoying than anything else. Actually, it's not even that
annoying. But is it a bug?

Thanks,

Sam ("Annoying") Buttrey
Naval Postgraduate School
buttrey at nps.navy.mil

