From: Peter.Lert at fmr.com (Lert, Peter)
Date: Thu, 09 Aug 2001 14:55:05 -0400
Subject: Distribution of Sample Means
Message-ID: <920376A1AF77D511BACC00508BE392506994C5@msgbos683nts.fmr.com>

My question has to do with being able to express the distribution of sample
means taken over a finite fraction f of the population.

Suppose I have a distribution on a finite space, with say 20 points. That
is, X can take one of 20 values Xi, with probability P(Xi) (note that
Sumi(P(Xi)) = 1 , where i = (1:20)). I want to know how to express the
distribution of a sample mean Mf = Sumi(Xi*p(Xi)) as a function of the
fraction f of the population contained in the sample.  That is, I want to
know how the mean of a random sample Mf depends on f and the underlying
distribution P(Xi) (or alternately properties of the distribution such as
the moments), where for a given sample Sumi(p(Xi)) = f, and 0 <= p(Xi) <=
P(Xi) for all i, and the values for p(Xi) are chosen randomly.  In the
application I am considering the typical values for f are 10-30%.

If I understand the function sample(), it will actually build a sample, but
that's not what I want.  I am hoping that someone knows how to express the
distribution of the sample mean, so that I can then compute with it in
SPlus.

Thanks for the help.

-- Peter Lert

