Subject: History in Linux version?
Message-ID: <3B73AB8C.BB8E1D3D@bgc-jena.mpg.de>

Dear Splus'ers,

Wonder if someone can enlighten me.  I've used Splus 3.3 for Windows for
several years and have come to depend on the history functionality of
Splus: appending executed command lines to a history file and being able
search backward through previous commands using F8 key or the History
menu.

If I recall correctly I used the following Splus.ini setup:
...
S_NOAUDIT=X
S_CMDFILE=+history.src
...

Which allowed appending of all executed command lines to one file in a
better readable form than in the audit file.

Now I switched to a different institute and have to work with a Linux
Splus version (Version 6.0 Release 1 for Linux 2.2.12 : 2000).  I hope
it is somehow possible to get the same history functionality in the
Linux version, can anybody help me out?
Sofar I have only found the use of _audit file and history() function or
command line editor, which are both rather limited in their
functionality.

Cheers
Peter Anthoni

