From zhang at calshp.cals.wisc.edu  Thu Aug 16 14:12:06 2001
From: zhang at calshp.cals.wisc.edu (weidong zhang)
Date: Thu, 16 Aug 2001 09:12:06 -0500
Subject: Summary of PCA question
Message-ID: <Pine.HPX.4.10.10108160910360.10448-100000@calshp.cals.wisc.edu>

My original questions:
1. I believe princomp() uses N-1 as denominator when calculating sample
variances, but I know some other sources uses N as denominator. I know
the
difference is minor when sample size is large. But N-1 is better
(unbiased) when sample size is small. So my understanding is that it
would
be good if we always use N-1 as denominator to calculate sample
variance.

2. We know if the scales are quite different between variables, we'd
better standardize the variables. Is it good if we always standardize
variables when we princomp() even the variables have similar scales? If
so, why s+ provide an option like corr to do this?

3.Why do some sources call the eigenvectors the loadings and other  
sources call the correlations (between the PCs and the original
variables) the loadings? What is the correct definitions for loadings
for
PCA? (SORRY for this non-S+ question)

Many thanks to Don Mckenzie, Michael Westphal,Peter Ellis and Matt
Kurbat for their responses.
Answers are :

Matt Kurbat
3. Eigenvectors for loadings are correct.  

However,when you estimate PCs (F) and loadings (L) on variables (X), you
get
        X=FL'

If you then regress X on F, you are solving for a beta matrix (b) via
least
squares
        X=Fb'

If the variables X and factors F have been standardized (mean 0,
variance
1),
then the regression coefficients (betas) ARE correlations

Don Mckenzie
3. Sources tend to be very confused about PCA and the difference between
it
and factor analysis.  The PCA loadings are the eigenvectors of the the
crossproduct matrix from your original data.  These are pre-multiplied
by
your data matrix to get the PCs.

The overheads from my Jan 9 lecture at:

http://www.cfr.washington.edu/classes.esc.521b/index.htm

Michael Westphal
2. Yes, standardize the vars and use the scale() command in S-Plus to do
so.
3. Eigenvectors, but that is roughly the same as the correlations.
Since PCA
is a mapping from some n dimensional space to a lower dimensional space,
the
eigenvectors correspond to the  "strengths" of theoriginal axes in
determining the newer axis.

Peter Ellis
2. On your question 2, I would not scale if the variables
are originally on similar scales _and_ there is some
sense in which I want to preserve the information that
maybe there is more variation in one variable than in
another.  For example, say each variable is a test
score out of 100.  Say that on the original scale, one
variable is realised all between 90 and 100, whereas
another variable ranges over the whole possible values
(0 to 100).  I would probably want to preserve in the
PCA solution the fact that most of the variance is
contained in the second variable; whereas scaling them
both first would (IMO) artificially magnify the
variance contained in the first scale, which tells us
little about the "real" variation between cases.  In
less straightforward cases, this becomes a matter for
