From therneau at mayo.edu  Fri Aug 17 21:37:46 2001
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Terry Therneau)
Date: Fri, 17 Aug 2001 16:37:46 -0500
Subject: Re: familty = poisson(ink = identity) in glm
In-Reply-To: <3B7D3E3B.9195C563@math.mun.ca>
Message-ID: <200108172137.QAA28759@rocky.mayo.edu>

 To fit the poisson model with an identity link, one has to prevent the code
from ever making a predicted rate yhat = X\beta that is <0.  For the usual
Poisson link function yhat = exp(X\beta), and no negative or zero values
occur, whatever the value of beta.  

  For many data sets, it is sufficient to use a decent starting value.  For
others, this is not enough: even though the solution is fine the iteration
path takes a step into the quicksand of negative predictions, and one 
such step is enough to kill it.

  I have fit such data using identity links successfully using the family
function below.  But before showing the example, one setup question is
in order.  The glm setup fits MLE models to y, the number of observed
events in each group.  Usually, however, we want to model the rate of
events as a function of covariates, not the total number.  With log links this
all works out:
        E(y_i) = rate_i * time_i
                = exp(X_i beta) * exp(log(t_i))
                = exp[X_i beta + log(t_i)]

Here t_i is the total observation time for the ith unit.  This rearrangement
is the reason for using offset(log(time)) in the Poisson model; log(t) appears
in the model as "just another covariate" but with known coefficient of 1.  
For linear fits it works out a bit different:
        E(y_i)  = X_i beta * t_i
                = (t_i X_i) beta
In order to model rates you have to premultiply each row of the X matrix
(each observation) by the observation time for that unit; at least
if you want the betas to refer to event rates.

  That given, here is an example of fitting a linear link.  The scaled
covariates (including the intercept) are in a matrix zz.

----------
# Standard link
fit5 <- glm(lung ~ age + pack + factor(bmicat) + offset(log(tpyrs)),
              data=rob, family=poisson, x=T)

# Identity link
#I need a better link function that stops the
#  NA's.  We use f(x) = x, for x > c
#                f(x) = c exp{(x-c)/c} for x <=c
#  for the inverse link function, i.e., rate = f(X beta).  
# This is linear for x>c, continuous with cont first derivatives, but stays
# positive. The corresponding link is c( 1+ log(y/c)) for y<c, and y for y>c
#

zz <- rob$tpyrs * fit5$x 
cutoff <- .0001
mylink <- list(names="Smooth ident",
               link = function(mu) 
                   ifelse(mu > cutoff, mu, cutoff*(1+ log(mu/cutoff))),
               inverse= function(eta)
                   ifelse(eta > cutoff, eta, cutoff*exp(eta/cutoff -1)),
               deriv = function(mu)
                   ifelse(mu > cutoff, 1, cutoff/mu),
               initialize = expression({ y<- as.numeric(y); mu <- y}))
myfamily <- make.family("Smooth", mylink, glm.variances[, 'mu'])

fit6 <- glm(lung ~ zz -1, data=rob, family=myfamily,
               start=fit5$fitted)

-----------------------------------------------------
 The value of "cutoff" that was used is completely arbitrary.  The data set
in question involved lung cancer rates as a function of age, pack years of
smoking, and categorized body mass index.  Using the standard fit (fit5)
for starting values turned out to be important.

