From bert_gunter at merck.com  Mon Aug 20 12:46:53 2001
From: bert_gunter at merck.com (Gunter, Bert)
Date: Mon, 20 Aug 2001 08:46:53 -0400
Subject: S-Plus inconsistency that can bite
Message-ID: <777F3F91D3F3D4118CCA00508B953E190195DE6B@usrymx06.merck.com>

All:

The numerical precision for S-Plus functions can vary. Occasionally, this
can cause real problems. Here is one example with lowess (in S-Plus 6.0 for
WIndows and S-Plus 200 for Windows. I haven't tried R).

>x<-seq(1,1+e-6, by=1e-8); y_rnorm(101)
> length(unique(x))
[1] 101   # unique is a double precision function
> z<- lowess(x,y)
> length(z$x)
[1]  9

apparently, lowess, which is an old function, works in single precision. The
newer loess(), however, apparently is double precision and so it works
properly.

While the above problem is easy to fix (use loess() or interpolate from the
results of lowess() ), it does seem to me to be a rather nasty and
undocumented trap. It certainly caught me.  May I suggest that lowess()
should be better documented and, perhaps,deprecated, or that it should be
fixed to work in double precision as most of the other S-Plus functions do.

Question: How many other single precision S-Plus functions are there? Are
they all at least documented?

Cheers,

