before even trying:  The simple message that R has no single precision
functions `by design', so I guess you won't have a problem.

Note that the ``missing'' as.single()  in the past lead to porting
difficulties which were remedied by pseudo-single type that can be used for
C / Fortran communication (but is not used really by any standard R code).

Here is your example, slightly enhanced, as it works fine in R :

  x <- seq(1,1+1e-6, by=1e-8)
  (n <- length(unique(x)))
  ##[1] 101   # unique is a double precision function
  y <- sin(6e6*(x-1))+ rt(n,df=2)/10
  plot(y ~ x)

  z <- lowess(x,y, f = .2)
  length(z$x)
  ## 101 as desired
  lines(z, col = 2)

