From jcp at research.bell-labs.com  Tue Aug 21 03:46:26 2001
From: jcp at research.bell-labs.com (Jose Pinheiro)
Date: Mon, 20 Aug 2001 23:46:26 -0400
Subject: 
Message-ID: <200108210346.XAA00294@jessie.research.bell-labs.com>

> After fitting 29  tree height data to a 3-parameter nonlinear function using
> nlsList,  I observed an estimated parameter from an individual (Tree 17) was
> large and wished to exclude the 3 parameters estimated for the tree from an
> interval plot. The plot()
> 
>          plot(intervals(Wb4.lis)[-c(17,46,75),])  
> 
> Wb4.lis is the fitted nls object while 17, 46, and 75 are row indices for
> the estimated 3 parameters to be excluded, resulted in an error:
> 
>       Error in [: Need 3 subscripts for array: .Dim=c(29, ..)
> 
> Any suggestions for excluding the parameters from plotting?

There are different ways that this can be accomplished, but not by
using a simple argument to the plot method, I am afraid. The error
message you are getting comes from the fact that the object returned
by intervals(Wb4.lis) is a three-way array (try it), and you are only
providing references to two indices. Since you only have 29 trees, I
am not sure why you are referencing the indices 17, 46 and 75. If
these are tree labels, you will need to use a different type of
indexing, like below.

One way to solve the problem is by eliminating the trees from the
original nlsList fit. You can do that using, for example, the argument
"subset" to nlsList, like

         subset = ~ is.na(match(Tree, c(17,46,75)))

A possibly simpler alternative is to do that with the output of
intervals, as you were hinting at. You can use

> Wb4.int <- intervals(Wb4.list)
> Wb4.grpName <- attr(Wb4.int, "groupsName")
> Wb4.int <- Wb4.int[is.na(match(dimnames(Wb4.int), c(17,46,75))),,]
> class(Wb4.int) <- "intervals.lmList"
> attr(Wb4.int, "groupsName") <- Wb4.grpName
> plot(Wb4.int)

The convoluted code is due to the fact that Wb4.int loses some of its
attributes in the subsetting. (If you are using S=PLUS 5.1 or 6.0, you
should use oldClass(Wb4.int) <- "intervals.lmList".) Also, referencing
the rows to be dropped by their actual number would make the code
simpler. 

Hope this helps.

--Jose'

