From sebuttre at nps.navy.mil  Tue Aug 21 19:34:28 2001
From: sebuttre at nps.navy.mil (Buttrey, Samuel)
Date: Tue, 21 Aug 2001 12:34:28 -0700
Subject: Re: Using blas.lib..."mystery" "solved"
Message-ID: <21255707C24105438C5BEA7061D33228332E53@essex.ad.nps.navy.mil>

Many thanks to Nick Ellis and Brian Ripley for help (above and beyond the
call) with my DLL problem. My problem was that I was trying to create a DLL
that called Clapack, and I was having trouble with blas.dll.

The problem is that the Clapack DLL imports symbols from blas.dll, and in
S-Plus 2000, anyway, there's a blas.dll in the "cmd" directory of the S-Plus
installation (that is, high up on the search path) so S-Plus looks for
Clapack's symbols there. Alas, they're not there; the blas.dll in S-Plus is
a Fortran one, and it wants to export symbols like "STPMV" while Clapack is
trying to import symbols like "stpmv_". I had my own blas.dll which exported
the correctly-named C-style symbols, but I couldn't persuade S-Plus to use
it (I don't have write access to the cmd directory and, anyway, I presume
S-Plus is using that DLL so I don't want to get rid of it). Messrs. Ellis
and Ripley were pointing me in this direction, but I was so blinded by
matching up the symbols I didn't think about the dll's name itself.

Obvious solution #1 -- use Fortran-based Lapack -- didn't work out for me.
More details are available on request.

Ridiculous solution #1 worked fine: I copied my blas.dll to blac.dll; then I
edited the lapack.dll (using vi, naturally -- I said this was ridiculous),
found the string "BLAS.dll", replaced it with "BLAC.dll" (hint -- stick with
four letters) and, as the British say, Bob was my uncle.

Of course my DLL still crashed S-Plus, but that's only to be expected.
Right?

Thanks again to Messrs. Ellis and Ripley,

Sam Buttrey
buttrey at nps.navy.mil

