Andrew - A few random thoughts:

- Showing only the significant interactions results in a subtle bias
- You can fairly easily make specific estimates of effects (contrasts)
  by forming differences in predicted values.  The difficulty as
  you have alluded is which contrasts are really meaningful.
- Sometimes representing the predicted values using rpart can
  result in a helpful interpretation of a complex model.  For
  example you might prune a tree predicting predict(fit) to
  within an R^2 of 0.95, and use that tree to show "big" effects.
  You would give all the raw predictors on the right hand side
  of the rpart model.

Frank Harrell

