The constant "e" is exp(1).  I can only imagine the reason why it is not
provided as a separate constant like pi is that all you ever want to do with
e is use it as the base of some power expression, e^x, in which case why not
use exp(x).

> 
> ii) Assume I fit a binary logistic regression model. Are there functions
> or is there an easy way to extract the CI for the Betas, the logit and
> the probability? For example, the logit and its confidence intreval can
> easily be determined in STATA using the lincom function. Is there
> something similar in S-Plus?

You can find standard errors easily enough and hence approximate confidence
intervals by inverting Wald tests.  For the regression coefficients you can
either fish them out of the summary() object or you can uses vcov(fit.mod)
to get the entire large-sample variance matrix.  (vcov is part of the MASS
library.)  There is also a function, confint, in the MASS library to find
possibly better confidence intervals for parameters by inverting the
likelihood ratio test.

For logits and probabilities you can get approximate standard errors using
predict(fit.mod, type = "link", se.fit = T) and predict(fit.mod, type =
"response", se.fit = T), respectively.

