I have come across a behavior involving source(), synchronize() and
scope that I don't understand. I would appreciate an explanation.
Splus v6 Solaris
Thanks
-Don

Here's an example. I have a small file of Splus commands named tmp.sp.
The file contains these lines:

## tmp.sp illustrates puzzle
blah <- numeric(4)
cat('blah',blah,'\n')
for (i in 1:4) {
   blah[i] <- i*2
   synchronize()
}
cat('blah',blah,'\n')

foob <- numeric(4)
cat('foob',foob,'\n')
for (i in 1:4) {
   foob[i] <- i*2
}
cat('foob',foob,'\n')
## end of tmp.sp

When I source() this file, and then type the names of the objects at
the Splus prompt, the value of foob is as expected, but that of blah
is not.
>  source('tmp.sp')
blah 0 0 0 0
blah 2 4 6 8
foob 0 0 0 0
foob 2 4 6 8
>  foob
[1] 2 4 6 8
>  blah
[1] 2 0 0 0

It makes no sense to me that the presence of synchronize() would
cause this value of blah after returning from source().

This is just a simple example that isolates the behavior. It arose in
a situation where the contents of the for() loop are much more
complex, and I wanted to save the current results at the end of each
iteration, in case of problems in a later iteration--as described in
?synchronize, which says:

"If you are doing a long computation iteratively and want to ensure
that permanent assignments will be committed now, regardless
of any error you may make in the future, synchronize() does that."

note,
>  version
Version 6.0 Release 1 for Sun SPARC, SunOS 5.6 : 2000

(In a brief search of Snews archives I didn't find anything, and
there are some hints in V&R, i.e, source() is a function "to which
