Thank you to Brian and Hong for their valuable information.
Aldi

It's integer overflow. By default, SPlus 6 treats numbers entered without
a
decimal point as integers, not as double-precision floating point
numbers.
On current 32-bit platforms, integers are stored using four bytes, which
allows a range of -2147483648 to 2147483647 (usually).

Put a decimal point in front of one of the numbers to force SPlus to
evaluate the computation in double precision:

> 15941358*20*8.0
[1] 2550617280

