Thank you to Bill and Chuck Taylor for their helpful information. Bill's
response gives further detail in how Splus in different versions and machines
treats integer numbers. I am thinking to share his email with the list members.

Aldi

Bill Dunlap wrote:

> On Thu, 23 Aug 2001, Aldi Kraja wrote:
>
> > Hi,
> > V. Splus6 for Unix: Is this a bug?
> >  15941358*20*8
> > [1] NA  <====================? ? ?
> > > 15941358*16
> > [1] 255061728
> >
> > I have to add a 0 by hand to find the correct result.
>
> In Splus6, if you don't have a decimal point at the end of a number
> it is treated as an integer.  In Splus2000 and before numbers were
> parsed as double precision, decimal point or not;  you had to use
> as.integer() or a few other functions like length() or ":" to get
> an integer.
>
> In Splus6, if integer multiplication or addition overflows (produces a
> result larger than 2^31-1 on a 32 bit machine, 2^63-1 on a  64 bit
> machine) then the result is changed to NA.  In Splus2000 and before you
> were likely to get a negative number (or a positive but incorrect one)
> after an overflow.
>
> Hence you can now get the correct result by adding a decimal point.
> (The "correct" result may be off a bit because double precision
> numbers have only 53 bits of precision, even though their range is
> much larger.)
>
> Splus6.0> 15941358. * 20. * 8.
> [1] 2550617280
>
> Note that in Splus4.x using as.integer() to force it to use integer
> arithmetic you would get
>
> Splus4.5> as.integer(15941358) * as.integer(20) * as.integer(8)
> [1] -1744350016
>
