From SH.Heisterkamp at rivm.nl  Mon Aug 27 09:07:53 2001
From: SH.Heisterkamp at rivm.nl (SH Heisterkamp)
Date: Mon, 27 Aug 2001 11:07:53 +0200
Subject: priggish behaviour of fix() ?
Message-ID: <OF5B9D0A0F.8E51DCAA-ONC1256AB5.002F1979@rivm.nl>

Dear S-Plus-list and people from helpful insightful
Some time ago I posted a question about what I called 'priggish behaviour
of fix()' I got very helpful response and I like to share it, as it was not
immediately clear what the right solution was. Thanks to David Smith, Bill
Dunlap, Steve McKinney, Nick Ellis and Dan Rie and a word of general advice
from Martin Maechler. To start with the last one:

.....,

shouldn't you know in the mean time, that Emacs + ESS is `the way'
and never use fix() ?

  ;-)

I'm only half joking:
We really teach people along this, here,
even when not using Emacs / ESS : We believe in
  ``the source   is the true representation'', as opposed to
  ``the S object is  "   "         "       ''

and strongly recommend people to work with <fname>.S
files both for defining functions and doing data analysis
(actually nowadays  <fname>.R , since we've switched to R almost
completely)

Regards,
Martin Maechler

===============
> #  what I typed in:   (note same.gender is True or False)
> #    ngen.cov <- !same.gender + 1
> #-- what S-plus made of the command after leaving fix()
>      ngen.cov <- !(same.gender + 1)      #
> something completely different!!!!!

help(Syntax) says that ! has a lower precedence than + and -
so the 2 expressions are equivalent.  The precedence order is

            $              component selectioHIGH
            [ [[           subscripts, elements
            ^              exponentiation
            -              unary minus
            :              sequence operator
            %anything%     special operator
            *   /          multiply, divide
            +   - ?        add, subtract
            <  >  <=  >=
              ==  !=       comparison
            !              not
            &   |  &&  ||  and, or
            ~              formulas
            <<-            assignment
            ->
            <-   _                          LOW

I did some tests that show they the parentheses that deparse()
inserts do not change the order of evaluation:

            > { !T + 1 }
            [1] F
            > { !(T+1) }
            [1] F
            > { !F + 1 }
            [1] F
            > { !(F+1) }
            [1] F
            > { !NA + 1 }
            [1] NA
            > { !(NA+1) }
            [1] NA

You may want (!same.gender)+1 or 1+!same.gender.

===========
The bottom line is: always be careful about the precedence of operators,
and in doubt, use between any pairs of ( ) another pair of () ....
As for the  advice of Martin Maechler, about function names and the use of
source instead of objects; for myself, I always name a function as
f.<name>, with the advantage that in listings with objects()  they are
grouped together. And when representing them as an ASCII-file they are
converted to <name>.f  (by a function called f.backup). BUT: it would have
been  of no help in this particular example, as source(<name>.f) would do
the conversion anyhow, as it was NOT a bug of fix(), but a consequence of
the precedence rules in S-Plus and probably also in R.

Thanks a lot anyone of you.

By the way in the end I simply used

     ngen.gov<- 2 - same.gender

more insightful...

Siem Heisterkamp

