On Mon, 27 Aug 2001, Jean V Adams wrote:

> I am interested in fitting a model relating a continuous response to two
> factors, their interaction, and a continuous predictor.  My primary
> interest is in testing to see if the continuous predictor has a significant
> effect on the response, after the main and interaction effects of the
> factors have been accounted for.  So, I fit a model like this:
>      fit <- aov(Y ~ A*B + X)
> Whether I use aov() or glm(), S-PLUS automatically orders the effects in
> the ANOVA table (which is output using summary(fit)) with the interaction
> term coming last.  Because my data come from an unbalanced design, order is
> important.  My question is, how can I override the default to order the
> effects in the ANOVA table the way *I* want them, with the continuous
> predictor coming last (A, B, A:B, X)?

The anova table is in the order of the fit, which you would find it hard
to alter.  You can do a anova on a list of fits.  So, a dummy test

A <- rep(factor(rep(letters[1:3], 4)),2)
B <- factor(rep(1:4, rep(6, 4)))
Y <- rnorm(24)
X <- 1:24

> anova(fit)
Analysis of Variance Table

Response: Y

Terms added sequentially (first to last)
          Df Sum of Sq  Mean Sq  F Value     Pr(F)
A          2  0.424708 0.212354 0.396988 0.6815981
B          3  4.737364 1.579121 2.952112 0.0797024
X          1  0.006604 0.006604 0.012346 0.9135296
A:B        6  4.320067 0.720011 1.346036 0.3162464
Residuals 11  5.884036 0.534912

Now try

fit2 <- aov(Y ~ A*B)
fit3 <- aov(Y ~ A)
fit4 <- aov(Y ~ 1)
anova(fit4, fit3, fit2, fit)

      Terms Resid. Df      RSS   Test Df Sum of Sq  F Value     Pr(F)
1         1        23 15.37278
2         A        21 14.94807         2  0.424708 0.396988 0.6815981
3     A * B        12  5.89064 +B+A:B  9  9.057431 1.881394 0.1602508
4 A * B + X        11  5.88404     +X  1  0.006604 0.012346 0.9135296

(and mine was balanced).

