From elmore at nssl.noaa.gov  Mon Aug 27 16:51:55 2001
From: elmore at nssl.noaa.gov (Kim Elmore)
Date: Mon, 27 Aug 2001 11:51:55 -0500
Subject: Best way to Approach Big Task
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.2.20010827112449.03b3aeb8@enterprise.nssl.noaa.gov>

Hi all,

I have a rather large task to accomplish.  I have some very simple-minded 
ways to approach it, but I'm in search of Enlightenment.  I'm using S-Plus 
2000 Release 3 on a dual-733 MHz P-III workstation with 512 MB of memory 
running Windows 2000 SP2, so the hardware is reasonably capable.

I have a 37901 x 201 data frame.  Each row consists of a time series of 
values at numerical model grid point.  I need to do some simple things on a 
big data set:

1) determine the lag-1 autocorrelation (rho) for each row of data,

2) use the mean of 1) to adjust the variance or each row of data to an 
estimate of the variance that reflects the time between effectively 
independent samples, and

3) find the confidence intervals for the mean value for each row of data 
(grid point) using a t distribution based on 201*(1-rho)/(1+rho) df.

Each column of data was initially provided to me as a single file.  I 
quickly learned to appreciate For loops (many thanks to Bill Venables and 
Brian Ripley for "S Programming") to read each file into the data frame, 
because a standard for loop quickly used up all the available memory for 
that process (2 GB).

Naively, I tried apply(<stuff>) even though I vaguely recall that apply() 
uses implicit loops. I found that it, too, slowly used up all of the 
available memory for the process, grinding everything to a halt. I *could* 
be lazy, and invoke the For loop again (similar to that described in 7.1 of 
"S Programming"), which is very intuitive for an old fortran hacker, but 
I'm wondering if there is a better way.

My main problem is memory, because if Windows 2000 is forced to page memory 
in and out very much, it will get agonizingly slow. I'll be relatively 
patient waiting for numerical computations as long as a CPU stays busy on 
the problem.

Cheers,

Kim Elmore
