From dsmith at insightful.com  Mon Aug 27 18:14:14 2001
From: dsmith at insightful.com (David Smith)
Date: Mon, 27 Aug 2001 11:14:14 -0700
Subject: Re: Best way to Approach Big Task
In-Reply-To: <5.1.0.14.2.20010827112449.03b3aeb8@enterprise.nssl.noaa.gov>
References: <5.1.0.14.2.20010827112449.03b3aeb8@enterprise.nssl.noaa.gov>
Message-ID: <LGEFIDJJDBKMHCAKBCOIGENODJAA.dsmith@insightful.com>

Memory, as Kim rightly points out, is the crucial issue.  S-PLUS 2000 can be
pretty poor at reclaiming no-longer-needed meory with each iteration of a
loop, and soon enough iterative procedures consume all the available RAM and
things run slowly.  Eventually they may consume all the swap too, and then
the computation fails for lack of memory.

The good news is that S-PLUS 6 is very good at reclaiming memory during
loops.  (This was one of the major things we focussed on for S-PLUS 6.) I've
seen several examples where the same code in S-PLUS 2000 causes memory to
linearly increase as the iterations progress, but in S-PLUS 6 the memory
requirements are constant.  So if one iteration works OK in the available
memory, then any number of iterations are OK.

[As an aside, the memory reclamation code can actually make the careful
S-PLUS 6 a little slower than the cavalier S-PLUS 2000 for tight loops on
small objects, but this is generally not noticeable.]

So my recommendation would be to use S-PLUS 6, and just use a simple for()
loop to iterate through the rows of the data frame (although you could also
use a timeSeries object in S-PLUS 6 which might be more convenient).

# David

