Thanks to Andy Liaw, Paul Rasmussen, and Brian Ripley for their help.

To specifically test for the effect of the continuous predictor, it was
suggested that I could fit an ANOVA on the full and reduced model, or that
I could simply use the function drop1().  However this approach alone does
not give a complete ANOVA table with the effects in the order I'd like.  It
was also suggested (by Prof. Ripley) that I could fit a series of reduced
models and use the anova() function to get the ANOVA table I was after.
This works nicely, and gave me the numbers I needed to check and see if my
klunky work around was doing what I wanted.

My work around was to represent the interaction factor as a factor in its
own right:
     ABint <- interaction(A, B)
I then fit the model
     fit <- aov(A + B + ABint + X)
When I looked at the fit
     fit
I got the comment that "3 out of 10 effects not estimable".  That makes
sense because A has three levels (2 df), B has two levels (1 df), and ABint
has six levels (5 df, of which I'm only interested in fitting 2, hence the
3 inestimable effects).  At this point, everything seemed to make sense,
but I wasn't absolutely sure I was getting the right numbers until I used
Prof. Ripley's approach as a comparison.  The work around worked, and
that's what I'll use since it requires less model fitting and fewer lines
of code.

Thanks again for the help.

